          "yrange": [-1.5, 1.5]
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "warn",
        "label": {
          "en": "⚠ Common Traps & Exam Pitfalls",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<strong>FTC Part 1 Chain Rule Missing:</strong> When differentiating an integral with a variable upper limit \\(u(x)\\):<br>\\[ \\frac{d}{dx}\\int_a^{u(x)} f(t)\\,dt = f(u(x)) \\cdot u'(x) \\]<br>Forgetting to multiply by \\(u'(x)\\) is the single most penalized error in AP Calculus!<br><br><strong>Lower Limit is a Function:</strong> If the variable is in the lower limit, reverse limits and negate first:<br>\\[ \\frac{d}{dx}\\int_{u(x)}^b f(t)\\,dt = -\\frac{d}{dx}\\int_b^{u(x)} f(t)\\,dt = -f(u(x))u'(x) \\]<br><strong>Both Limits Variable:</strong> Split at a constant \\(c\\): \\(\\int_{v(x)}^{u(x)} = \\int_c^{u(x)} - \\int_c^{v(x)}\\), yielding \\(f(u(x))u'(x) - f(v(x))v'(x)\\).",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "practiceProblem",
        "question": {
          "en": "Find the derivative \\(h'(x)\\) for the accumulation function \\(h(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_2^{x^3} \\sqrt{1 + t^2}\\,dt\\).",
          "ar": null
        },
        "solution": {
          "en": "By FTC Part 1 and the Chain Rule, let \\(u = x^3\\) so that \\(\\frac{du}{dx} = 3x^2\\). Then:<br>\\[ h'(x) = \\sqrt{1 + (x^3)^2} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x^3) = 3x^2 \\sqrt{1 + x^6}. \\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      }
    ]
  },
